---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import HoursCalculator from '@components/islands/HoursCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'hours-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How do I calculate hours worked?',
    answer:
      'Enter your start and end times and any unpaid break. The calculator subtracts the break from the elapsed time and shows the result in hours and minutes, plus decimal hours for payroll.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many work hours are in a year?',
    answer:
      'Full-time work is usually counted as 2,080 hours a year — 40 hours a week × 52 weeks. That works out to about 173 hours in a month. Paid time off does not reduce the figure; unpaid leave does.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many hours is full-time work?',
    answer:
      'Full-time is most commonly 40 hours a week (8 hours over 5 days), though some employers and countries treat 35–37.5 hours as full-time. Check your contract for the definition that applies to you.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does it handle overnight shifts?',
    answer:
      'Yes. If the end time is earlier than the start time, the calculator assumes the shift crossed midnight and counts the hours correctly.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What are decimal hours?',
    answer:
      'Decimal hours express minutes as a fraction of an hour — for example, 8 hours 30 minutes is 8.5 decimal hours. Payroll systems usually use this format to multiply by an hourly rate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I turn hours worked into pay?',
    answer:
      'Multiply the decimal hours by your hourly rate. For example, 8.5 decimal hours at $20/hour is $170. To convert between hourly, weekly and annual pay, use the salary calculator.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-04' }}
  intro="Calculate the hours between two times, minus breaks — including overnight shifts. Get the result in hours and minutes and as decimal hours for payroll."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <HoursCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use the hours calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your <strong>start time</strong> and <strong>end time</strong> in 24-hour format.</li>
      <li>Add any unpaid <strong>break</strong> in minutes (leave it at zero if there is none).</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate Hours</strong> to see the result in hours-and-minutes and as decimal hours; it updates as you adjust the figures.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the calculation works</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator finds the minutes between your start and end times, then subtracts the
      break. That total is shown two ways: as hours and minutes for reading, and as decimal
      hours for payroll. If a break is longer than the shift itself, the result is simply zero
      rather than a negative time.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Clock in at <strong>9:00</strong>, out at <strong>17:30</strong>, with a
      <strong>45-minute</strong> unpaid lunch. The gap is 8 hours 30 minutes (510 minutes);
      take away 45 minutes and you have <strong>7 hours 45 minutes</strong> of paid time, which
      is <strong>7.75 decimal hours</strong>. At $20 an hour that shift is 7.75 × 20 = $155.
    </p>

    <h2>Overnight shifts</h2>
    <p>
      When the end time is earlier than the start time, the calculator assumes the shift crossed
      midnight and adds a day before subtracting. A shift from <strong>22:00 to 06:00</strong>
      therefore comes out as a full <strong>8 hours</strong>, not a negative span, so night and
      swing shifts need no special handling on your part.
    </p>

    <h2>What decimal hours are</h2>
    <p>
      Decimal hours express the minutes as a fraction of an hour, which is the format most
      payroll systems multiply by an hourly rate. Thirty minutes is 0.5, not 0.30. The common
      conversions:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Decimal hour</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>10 min</td><td>0.17</td></tr>
        <tr><td>15 min</td><td>0.25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>20 min</td><td>0.33</td></tr>
        <tr><td>30 min</td><td>0.50</td></tr>
        <tr><td>45 min</td><td>0.75</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How many work hours are in a year?</h2>
    <p>For standard full-time work at 40 hours a week, the common reference figures are:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Period</th><th>Full-time hours (40/week)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Week</td><td>40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Month</td><td>~173</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Year</td><td>2,080</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Turning hours into pay</h2>
    <p>
      To estimate pay, multiply the decimal hours by your hourly rate — 8.5 decimal hours at
      $20/hour is $170. To convert between hourly, weekly and annual figures, use the
      <a href="/finance/salary-calculator">salary calculator</a>; to add and subtract spans of
      time rather than clock times, see the <a href="/everyday/time-calculator">time
      calculator</a>. This tool is for general timekeeping — always follow your employer's
      official rounding and timekeeping rules for payroll.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
